i don't remember the year or the moment  exactly but i do remember thinking  what do i really believe in what  do i want the future to look like for  future generations  and it did not take me long to focus in  on the things that i truly believe in  are my faith and the other close second  was hillsdale college  [Music]  my route to hillsdale is a a bit of an  interesting one because while i was  i would definitely say it was one of if  not the best decisions of my life  it was one of those that i made  unlike me without a lot of angst  and overthinking i majored in  english the greatest growth i  experienced while i was on campus  was definitely in the area of confidence  confidence is something that i think i  struggled with  as one of six kids in a family are  always trying to figure out who you are  and  where you fit in so this was the first  thing that  i had chosen to do that was different  than anyone else in my family had chosen  to do i was paving my own path  and at first i felt tentative about that  and by the time i got to  my senior year i felt confident about  who i was what my strengths were  quite frankly what my weaknesses were  but also i knew that i could forge my  own path  i felt confident i could go from there  to a new environment in my postgraduate  life  and be successful as well i started my  career with  the chicago bulls as an intern and  the rest is history  having grown up in newport michigan  which is a  small town my entry into chicago was  a bit overwhelming the city felt massive  to me i had to figure things out by  myself sink or swim  and i learned by trial and error and i  kept feeling that confidence that i  started to bill at hillsdale grove  we knew at that time going into the 96  97 season still had michael jordan  scottie pippen dennis rodman we knew we  were going to win a championship  we didn't say that but we knew my first  day with the chicago bulls remember  sitting down  with the head of the pr department and  he said there's a  big party going on here and you are part  of it and it's exciting  but you'll be parking the cars outside  and every day of my career i've  remembered that because  it reminds me every day to know your  role  and play that role as best as you  possibly can  [Music]  so fast forward several years and i'm in  my fifth year  with the chicago marathon doing pr and  as luck or miss fortune would have it  the weather on the day of the marathon  was  oppressively hot and we knew it was  going to be a bad day  we canceled at midstream due to mostly  the health of the back  of the pack the next year of my life as  a result of that was  personally and professionally very  painful because we were under a ton  of media criticism my phone rang  non-stop every day all day for  months and it completely changed my  perspective quite frankly i became very  burned out  from working with the media as a result  of that and what i've realized is when  you are in moments of pain  you are growing i was introduced to  working with an organization called  octagon  sports marketing and knew that if anyone  was going to take a chance  on converting a pr person to a marketing  person it would be the people that i met  at octagon and they did they offered me  the opportunity and i have been with  them for the last 10 years  what's really enticed me to maintain a  career in sports for as long as i have  is that team environment i realize i am  at my best  when i'm surrounded by other people who  are really intelligent  and very talented and very skilled and i  know i have a role to play at that table  and so being part of that team that  achieves something remarkable  is is the personal thrill i get from  working in this industry  one of the things i always wanted to be  part of was  giving back to my communities and  organizations that i care about  i had been volunteering with the college  probably from near on the moment that i  graduated  i believe in its mission now more than i  did even when i was on campus and  and i believed in it then and at one  point  i remember gregor haystead who was the  head of alumni relations at the time  said would you ever consider joining the  alumni board  and my response was would the alumni  board ever consider  allowing me membership of course  and i'm finishing up my eighth year of  service on the board and i've  enjoyed it immensely you know once i  got onto the board we looked at new ways  to get  alumni involved on campus for the two  years that i was president  it felt very much like any other  consulting meeting i would go into with  a client  and i'm a problem solver so i'm looking  at college objectives and saying here  are the 10 things i think we can achieve  and help you with over the next 10 years  it furthers my belief that  things all come together at the right  time i think i was the  the right person at that time to be  sitting at the table  and talking about how we could shift and  change and mold the board's role  probably the most beautiful part of the  board is a  very familiar feel of family similar to  debates that you might have on campus we  have those debates in the boardroom  at the end of the day we all still have  such an amazing time together  it reinvigorated my relationships  through the college you would think that  we've been  friends for decades it's it's one of  my favorite ways to spend my free time
 

